  [OK] sys/riscv/include/sbi.h:171-175  sbi_probe_extension
      This is a one-line inline wrapper over the SBI BASE_PROBE_EXTENSION ecall, which is the RISC-V SBI specification's defined mechanism for S-mode software to discover M-mode firmware capabilities. The SBI spec does not mandate cryptographic attestation for extension probing — M-mode is architecturally the root of trust for S-mode. This code correctly implements the spec. The claimed connection to RPMI BOOTSTATUS_SET_STATUS is a category error: SBI extension probing (ecall-based, privilege boundary) and RPMI boot-status reporting (shared-memory transport, platform management) are entirely different mechanisms from different specifications. FreeBSD has no RPMI code whatsoever.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/mp_machdep.c:327-414  cpu_init_fdt
      Standard AP boot orchestration: probes SBI HSM extension, starts harts via sbi_hsm_hart_start(), spin-waits for APs, and logs progress via printf(). The printf() calls at lines 384 and 403 emit diagnostic messages to the kernel console (UART/SBI putchar), not to a platform microcontroller via RPMI shared-memory transport. There is no BOOTSTATUS_SET_STATUS invocation, no RPMI service group discovery, no platform-management message construction. This is ordinary kernel boot logging, not an untrusted-information-flow to remote management consumers. The FreeBSD RISC-V port contains no RPMI client code, so the BOOTSTATUS_SET_STATUS vulnerability pattern is inapplicable here.
  [OK] sys/riscv/include/sbi.h:145-168  sbi_call
      This is the raw RISC-V `ecall` inline assembly implementing the S-mode-to-M-mode privilege boundary crossing as defined by the RISC-V ISA and SBI specification. The ecall instruction is the architecturally mandated mechanism for S-mode to request M-mode services; there is no 'missing authentication envelope' because M-mode is the higher-privilege root of trust by definition. M-mode controls what S-mode can access via PMP, and M-mode firmware is free to implement its own access-control policies. This is correct per the RISC-V privilege architecture. The BOOTSTATUS_SET_STATUS vulnerability concerns RPMI shared-memory transport between an AP and a platform microcontroller (BMC), which uses a completely different mechanism from SBI ecalls.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/sbi.c:289-347  sbi_init
      SBI initialization per the SBI specification: queries spec version, implementation IDs, mvendorid/marchid/mimpid, then probes for legacy-replacement extensions (TIME, IPI, RFNC, SRST) and falls back to legacy extension probing with KASSERT guards. Extension flags (has_time_extension, has_srst_extension, etc.) are set based on firmware responses and used only to select the correct SBI call convention (legacy vs. replacement). This is correct SBI discovery. The claim that this 'models trust-without-verification for RPMI boot-status services' is unfounded: RPMI services use dedicated shared-memory transport with their own discovery mechanism (RPMI service group announcements), not SBI extension probing. No RPMI service group is probed or enabled in this code.
  [OK] sys/riscv/riscv/mp_machdep.c:113-148  release_aps
